// Unit 2 "Stocks", Chapter 3: Value Investing
export default {
  id: 'stocks-ch3',
  title: 'Value Investing',
  blurb: 'Think like a business owner: separate price from value, demand a margin of safety, find moats, estimate intrinsic value, and dodge value traps.',
  lessons: [
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
    {
      id: 'stocks-3-price-vs-value',
      title: 'Price vs Value and the Moody Mr. Market',
      summary: 'Meet Benjamin Graham, the father of value investing, his famous Mr. Market parable, and the line between investing and speculating.',
      minutes: 13,
      icon: 'book',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The garage sale lesson', html: `<p>At a garage sale, Maya spots a vintage espresso machine tagged <strong>$40</strong>. She works at a café, so she knows the model: restored ones sell for around <strong>$250</strong>. She buys it on the spot.</p><p>The seller was not "wrong" about the price. The price was simply whatever the seller asked. The <em>value</em> was what the machine was actually worth to someone who understood it. Those are two different numbers, and the gap between them is where Maya's bargain came from.</p><p>That is the core idea of <span class="term" data-def="An approach to investing that estimates what a business is really worth and buys only when the market price is well below that estimate.">value investing</span>:</p><p><span class="hl">Price is what you pay. Value is what you get.</span></p><p>A stock's price is set every second by buyers and sellers. Its value depends on the cash the business will produce over its life. Most days they are not far apart. Sometimes they are very far apart, and value investors spend their time hunting for those moments.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Same espresso machine, two numbers. The price tag and what it is really worth. Value investors live in the gap." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Meet Benjamin Graham', html: `<p><strong>Benjamin Graham</strong> is often called the father of value investing. He was an investor and a professor at Columbia Business School, and he lived through the 1929 crash and the Great Depression, which shaped his obsession with not losing money.</p><p>He wrote two of the most influential investing books ever:</p><ul><li><strong>Security Analysis</strong> (1934, with David Dodd), a dense guide to analyzing companies.</li><li><strong>The Intelligent Investor</strong> (first published in 1949), written for everyday investors.</li></ul><p>His most famous student was <strong>Warren Buffett</strong>, who studied under Graham at Columbia and later worked at his investment firm. Buffett went on to build Berkshire Hathaway into a giant and has repeatedly credited The Intelligent Investor as the best book on investing he has read.</p><p>Graham's big idea: treat a stock as a piece of a real business, not a ticker that wiggles. Then the question becomes, what is this business worth, and is the price well below that?</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Graham taught that a stock is part ownership of a business. Sounds obvious. Most market chatter forgets it daily." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The parable of Mr. Market', html: `<p>In The Intelligent Investor, Graham told a story that investors still repeat. Imagine you own a share of a private business with a partner named <strong>Mr. Market</strong>. Every single day, Mr. Market shows up and offers to either buy your share or sell you his, at a price he names.</p><p>Here is the thing about Mr. Market: he is emotional. Some days he is euphoric and names a ridiculously high price. Other days he is gloomy and offers to sell for far less than the business is worth. The business itself barely changes from day to day. His mood does.</p><p>Graham's point: <span class="hl">Mr. Market is there to serve you, not to guide you</span>. You are never forced to trade with him. When his price is silly-high, you can sell to him. When it is silly-low, you can buy. And on most days, you can simply ignore him.</p><p>The danger is letting his mood become your mood.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'You have carefully estimated a business is worth about $50 a share. Mr. Market, in a panic, offers to sell you shares at $28. What would Graham suggest?', options: ['Sell your shares too, since the market knows best', 'Consider buying, if your analysis still holds', 'Wait until the price rises above $50 to confirm', 'Ignore your analysis and follow the trend'], answer: 1, explain: 'Graham saw a gloomy Mr. Market as an opportunity. If your estimate of value is still sound, a panicked low price is the chance to buy, not a signal to flee.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Mr. Market in real life', html: `<p>Mr. Market's mood swings are not just a story. Look at a few historical episodes:</p><ul><li><strong>The dot-com bubble (late 1990s to 2002):</strong> investors paid enormous prices for internet companies with little or no profit, then the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite lost nearly 80% from its 2000 peak.</li><li><strong>The 2008 financial crisis:</strong> fear spread so widely that shares of many solid businesses were dumped alongside the truly broken banks.</li><li><strong>Early 2020:</strong> as the pandemic hit, the S&P 500 fell about a third in roughly a month, then recovered to new highs within months.</li></ul><p>In each case, the price of many businesses moved far more than their long-term value. That does not mean every crash is a bargain. Some businesses really did lose value. The skill is telling the difference, and having the cash and the nerve to act.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Crashes look like obvious bargains in hindsight. In real time they feel terrifying, and some prices keep falling. Nerve without analysis is just gambling." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'According to Graham, the daily stock price is the best guide to what your share of the business is worth.', answer: false, explain: "Graham taught the opposite. The daily price reflects Mr. Market's mood. Your estimate of the business value should guide you, and the price is just an offer you can accept or ignore." },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Investing vs speculating', html: `<p>Graham drew a sharp line. In his view, an operation counts as <strong>investing</strong> only if, after thorough analysis, it promises reasonable safety of your money and an adequate return. Anything that does not meet that bar is <span class="term" data-def="Buying mainly in hope of a price rise, without solid analysis of the underlying value or protection against loss.">speculation</span>.</p><p>Compare two people:</p><ul><li><strong>Dev</strong> buys a stock because it is trending online and "has momentum." He has not read a single filing. He plans to sell when someone pays more.</li><li><strong>Maya</strong> studies a company's statements, estimates its value, and buys only when the price is well below it, expecting to hold for years.</li></ul><p>Dev is speculating. Maya is investing. Graham did not say speculation was illegal or evil. He said people get hurt when they speculate while <em>believing</em> they are investing, and when they bet money they cannot afford to lose.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each Graham idea to its meaning.', pairs: [ { left: 'Mr. Market', right: 'A moody partner whose daily prices you can use or ignore' }, { left: 'Investing', right: 'Thorough analysis, safety of principal, adequate return' }, { left: 'Speculation', right: 'Buying mainly on hope that the price will rise' }, { left: 'Value', right: 'What the business is really worth' } ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The honest counterpoint', html: `<p>Before you think beating the market is easy, here is the other side. For big, heavily followed companies, thousands of professionals are analyzing the same numbers. Prices are usually <em>reasonably</em> close to a sensible value. That is the idea behind the <span class="term" data-def="The theory that market prices quickly reflect available information, making it hard to consistently beat the market.">efficient market hypothesis</span>.</p><p>The evidence is humbling. Studies such as S&P's long-running SPIVA reports have found that most actively managed US stock funds trail their benchmark index over long periods, after fees. These are full-time professionals.</p><p>So why learn value investing at all? Because the mindset protects you even if you mostly own index funds: it teaches you to ignore hype, think in businesses, stay calm in crashes, and avoid overpaying. Those habits matter more than any single stock pick.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'wow', say: "Even Buffett has said most people are better off in low-cost index funds. Thinking like an owner still helps you stay calm." } },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'Put a value investor\'s basic process in a sensible order.', items: ['Understand how the business makes money', 'Estimate what the business is worth', 'Compare that value with the market price', 'Buy only if the price is well below value', 'Hold while the business story stays intact'], explain: 'Value investing starts with the business, not the price. Only after estimating value do you look at what Mr. Market is offering.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Value investing', back: 'Buying businesses for well below a careful estimate of what they are worth.' }, { front: 'Intrinsic value', back: 'What a business is really worth based on the cash it will produce.' }, { front: 'Mr. Market', back: "Graham's parable of an emotional partner offering daily prices." }, { front: 'Speculation', back: 'Buying on hope of a price rise without solid analysis or protection.' }, { front: 'Efficient market hypothesis', back: 'The idea that prices quickly reflect available information.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Which book did Benjamin Graham write for everyday investors?', options: ['The Wealth of Nations', 'Security Analysis', 'The Intelligent Investor', 'One Up on Wall Street'], answer: 2, explain: 'The Intelligent Investor (1949) was aimed at general investors. Security Analysis (1934, with David Dodd) was the more technical text.' },
          { q: 'In the Mr. Market parable, what should you do when Mr. Market is euphoric and offers a very high price?', options: ['Buy more, because he is optimistic', 'Consider selling to him if the price is far above value', 'Panic and sell everything else too', 'Report him to the SEC'], answer: 1, explain: 'A euphoric Mr. Market offering a silly-high price gives you the option to sell. You use his moods; you do not copy them.' },
          { q: 'Dev buys a stock because it is trending online, without analyzing the business. In Graham terms, he is...', options: ['Investing with a margin of safety', 'Value investing', 'Hedging', 'Speculating'], answer: 3, explain: 'Without thorough analysis and protection against loss, Graham would call it speculation.' },
          { q: 'What has research such as the SPIVA reports generally found about active US stock funds?', options: ['Most trail their benchmark index over long periods', 'Most beat the index every year', 'They always match the index exactly', 'They outperform during every bull market'], answer: 0, explain: 'Over long periods, most active funds have lagged their benchmarks after fees, a reminder that beating the market is hard.' },
          { q: 'Why is the gap between price and value important to value investors?', options: ['Because price and value are always identical', 'Because buying well below value is where the bargain and protection come from', 'Because value only matters for bonds', 'Because price never changes'], answer: 1, explain: 'Value investors look for prices well below their estimate of value. That gap is both the potential return and the cushion against mistakes.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
    {
      id: 'stocks-3-margin-of-safety',
      title: 'Margin of Safety: Room to Be Wrong',
      summary: 'Graham\'s most important idea: buy well below your estimate of value, so mistakes and bad luck do not sink you.',
      minutes: 13,
      icon: 'shield',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Build the bridge stronger than the trucks', html: `<p>Graham liked an engineering comparison. When engineers design a bridge, they do not build it to hold <em>exactly</em> the heaviest truck they expect. If trucks weigh 10 tons, they build a bridge that can hold far more. Why? Because their estimates could be off, steel can have flaws, and someday a truck will be overloaded.</p><p>Investing works the same way. Your estimate of a business's value is just that, an estimate. You might be too optimistic. A recession might hit. A competitor might show up. So you leave room for error.</p><p>Graham called this the <span class="term" data-def="The gap between your estimate of a stock's intrinsic value and the price you pay, which cushions you against errors and bad luck.">margin of safety</span>, and he treated it as the central idea of sound investing. If you only remember one thing from this chapter, remember this one.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: "Never build a bridge that can only hold exactly the trucks you expect. Same goes for your portfolio." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'margin-of-safety', caption: 'The gap between estimated intrinsic value and the price you pay is your cushion. The bigger the gap, the more you can be wrong and still be okay.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Measuring the cushion', html: `<p>The margin of safety is usually expressed as a percentage of your value estimate:</p><p><span class="hl">Margin of safety = (estimated value - price) / estimated value</span></p><p>Maya studies Lumen Bikes and estimates it is worth about <strong>$50 a share</strong>. The stock trades at <strong>$35</strong>.</p><p>Margin = ($50 - $35) / $50 = $15 / $50 = <strong>30%</strong>.</p><p>That means Maya's value estimate could be 30% too high and she would still only be paying a fair price. If her estimate is right, she has bought a dollar of value for 70 cents.</p><p>Notice what this does to the odds. Buying at a big discount lowers how far the price can sensibly fall and raises how far it can rise if the market comes around to fair value. Lower risk and higher potential return, at the same time. In most of finance, those two fight each other. Here, a low price helps both.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Leo estimates Pebble Coffee is worth $80 a share. It trades at $60. What is his margin of safety, in percent?', answer: 25, tolerance: 0.1, unit: '%', explain: '($80 - $60) / $80 = $20 / $80 = 25%.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why your estimate will be wrong', html: `<p>Here is an uncomfortable truth: your value estimate will almost never be exactly right. Maybe it will be a little too high, maybe a lot too low. That is not a sign you are bad at this. It is the nature of predicting the future.</p><p>Things that commonly go wrong:</p><ul><li><strong>Growth disappoints.</strong> You assumed sales would grow 10% a year. They grow 4%.</li><li><strong>Margins shrink.</strong> A new competitor forces price cuts.</li><li><strong>Bad luck.</strong> A recession, a supply shock, a lawsuit.</li><li><strong>You missed something.</strong> A footnote in the 10-K about debt coming due.</li></ul><p>The margin of safety does not require you to predict which of these will happen. It just assumes <em>something</em> will. If you buy at a deep enough discount, ordinary disappointments still leave you with a fair outcome. Only a true disaster sinks you.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "A margin of safety is humility turned into a number. It admits you might be wrong before the market proves it." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Maya estimates a business is worth $60 a share and insists on a 30% margin of safety. What is the highest price she should pay, in dollars?', answer: 42, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: 'She wants to pay at most 70% of value: $60 x (1 - 0.30) = $42.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'How big should the margin be?', html: `<p>There is no official number. It depends on how confident you can reasonably be:</p><ul><li><strong>Stable, predictable business</strong> (say, a company selling toothpaste for decades): your estimate is likely closer to reality, so a smaller margin may be acceptable.</li><li><strong>Uncertain business</strong> (a young tech company, a cyclical miner, a company with heavy debt): your estimate could be wildly off, so you would want a much bigger margin.</li></ul><p>Graham himself, in the decades after the Great Depression, often bought companies trading below their <strong>net current assets</strong>: cash, receivables and inventory, minus <em>all</em> liabilities. These "net-nets" were so cheap that the long-term assets came free. Such bargains are rare among larger companies today, but the principle remains: the shakier your estimate, the bigger the discount you need.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "A 40% discount on a value you made up is not a margin of safety. The cushion is only as real as the estimate underneath it." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'myth', title: 'Myth: A stock that fell 50% has a 50% margin of safety', html: `<p>A falling price creates a margin of safety only if the <em>value</em> did not fall too. If a company lost its biggest customer, its value may have dropped even more than its price. Then the "discount" is an illusion. The margin is always measured against your fresh estimate of what the business is worth today, never against where the stock used to trade.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Which situation calls for the LARGEST margin of safety?', options: ['A decades-old consumer staples company with steady profits', 'A utility with regulated, predictable earnings', 'A young company with one product, heavy debt and no profits yet', 'A large, diversified food company with little debt'], answer: 2, explain: 'The more uncertain your estimate, the more room for error you need. A young, indebted, unprofitable company is the hardest to value.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Safety beyond the price', html: `<p>Price is the main margin of safety, but value investors stack other cushions too:</p><ul><li><strong>A strong balance sheet.</strong> Little debt and plenty of cash mean a company can survive a bad stretch without desperate moves.</li><li><strong>No borrowed money.</strong> Investing with borrowed money can force you to sell at the worst time. Without leverage, you can wait.</li><li><strong>Diversification and position sizing.</strong> If no single holding can ruin you, one wrong estimate is survivable.</li><li><strong>A personal cushion.</strong> An emergency fund means you never have to sell stocks in a crash to pay rent.</li></ul><p>That last one matters more than people think. The best value investment in the world is useless if you are forced to sell it at the bottom.</p><p>Notice that none of these cushions make you money directly. They buy you something better: the ability to wait, which is often what a cheap stock needs before its price catches up with its value.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'penny', mood: 'happy', say: "Your emergency fund is a margin of safety for your whole life. It lets your investments stay invested when things get bumpy." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If you buy a stock with a 30% margin of safety, you cannot lose money on it.', answer: false, explain: 'A margin of safety lowers the odds and size of losses, but your value estimate could be badly wrong, or the business could deteriorate. It is protection, not a guarantee.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'Leo was wrong, and it was fine', html: `<p>Leo estimated a furniture retailer was worth $40 a share and bought at $26, a 35% margin. Then a housing slowdown hit and he revised his estimate down to $32. His estimate of value had dropped by 20%. But because he paid $26, he still owned shares worth more than he paid by his new estimate. Someone who bought the same stock at $40, the "fair" price, was now sitting on a loss. Same mistake, very different outcome.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Margin of safety', back: 'The discount between estimated value and the price you pay.' }, { front: 'Net-net', back: 'A stock priced below its current assets minus all liabilities. A classic Graham bargain.' }, { front: 'Leverage', back: 'Investing with borrowed money. Magnifies gains and losses, and can force sales.' }, { front: 'Position sizing', back: 'Deciding how much to put in each investment so one mistake cannot sink you.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'A stock is estimated to be worth $100 and trades at $70. What is the margin of safety?', options: ['70%', '43%', '30%', '100%'], answer: 2, explain: '($100 - $70) / $100 = 30%.' },
          { q: 'What is the main purpose of a margin of safety?', options: ['To guarantee a profit', 'To cushion against errors in your estimate and bad luck', 'To make sure you buy at the exact bottom', 'To avoid paying taxes'], answer: 1, explain: 'It gives you room to be wrong. It cannot guarantee profits or perfect timing.' },
          { q: 'You estimate a company is worth $50 and want a 40% margin of safety. What is the highest price you would pay?', options: ['$30', '$40', '$20', '$35'], answer: 0, explain: '$50 x (1 - 0.40) = $30.' },
          { q: 'Why should a highly uncertain business require a larger margin of safety?', options: ['Because the SEC requires it', 'Because uncertain businesses always fail', 'Because they pay higher dividends', 'Because your value estimate is more likely to be badly wrong'], answer: 3, explain: 'The less predictable the business, the wider the range of possible values, so you need a bigger cushion.' },
          { q: 'Which of these is NOT a margin-of-safety habit?', options: ['Keeping an emergency fund', 'Buying with heavy borrowed money to maximize returns', 'Preferring companies with strong balance sheets', 'Sizing positions so one mistake cannot sink you'], answer: 1, explain: 'Leverage removes safety. It can force you to sell at the worst time and magnifies losses.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
    {
      id: 'stocks-3-economic-moats',
      title: 'Economic Moats: What Keeps Rivals Out',
      summary: 'Why some companies keep earning high profits for decades while others get competed away. The five classic moat sources, and how moats erode.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'lock',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Every castle needs a moat', html: `<p>Ortiz Bakeries earns about a 21% return on the capital invested in it. That is great. It is also an invitation. If you can make 21% a year selling croissants, someone will open a bakery across the street, then another, until prices drop and returns sink toward average.</p><p>That is how competition works, and it is why most high profits do not last. The question every long-term investor asks is: <em>what stops the competitors?</em></p><p>Warren Buffett popularized a medieval image for the answer. A great business is a castle. Its profits are the treasure inside. An <span class="term" data-def="A durable competitive advantage that protects a company's profits from competitors for many years.">economic moat</span> is whatever keeps attackers out: something rivals cannot easily copy, no matter how much money they throw at it.</p><p>A wide moat lets a company earn high returns on capital for years, even decades. And that, compounded over time, is where a lot of long-term value comes from.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Great profits attract competitors like a picnic attracts ants. A moat is what keeps the ants out." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'moat', caption: 'The business castle sits behind a moat made of advantages rivals cannot easily copy. Notice that a moat can be wide or narrow, and can shrink over time.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Moat 1: network effects', html: `<p>A <span class="term" data-def="When a product becomes more valuable to each user as more people use it.">network effect</span> happens when each new user makes the product more valuable for everyone else.</p><p>Think about a marketplace app. Buyers go where the most sellers are. Sellers go where the most buyers are. Once one marketplace has both, a newcomer with a slightly better app still struggles, because it has nobody on it. Social networks, messaging apps and payment card networks work similarly: a card is more useful when more stores accept it, and stores accept it because so many people carry it.</p><p>Network effects can be incredibly powerful, often leading to one or two dominant players. But they are not permanent. If users find a reason to move together, the effect can unravel in reverse, faster than anyone expects. Early social networks that once seemed unbeatable lost their users to newer rivals.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Moat 2: switching costs', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="The time, money, hassle or risk a customer faces when changing from one product or provider to another.">Switching costs</span> are the pain a customer feels when changing providers. The higher the pain, the more a company can keep customers, and often raise prices a little, without losing them.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li><strong>Business software</strong> that a company's whole accounting team is trained on, holding years of data. Switching means retraining, migration and risk of errors.</li><li><strong>Your bank account</strong>, with automatic payments, direct deposit and saved payees all set up.</li><li><strong>A phone ecosystem</strong> where your apps, photos and accessories are tied together.</li></ul><p>Ms. Ortiz has a small version: cafés that buy her wholesale bread have built their menus around it. Switching suppliers means new recipes, taste tests and annoyed regulars. It is a narrow moat, but it is real.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: "A hospital uses one company's software to store all patient records and train all staff. The vendor raises prices 5% a year and almost no hospitals leave. Which moat is this?", options: ['Network effect', 'Efficient scale', 'Switching costs', 'Cost advantage'], answer: 2, explain: 'Moving years of records and retraining every employee is costly and risky. That pain is a switching cost, and it gives the vendor pricing power.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Moats 3 and 4: cost and scale', html: `<p><strong>Cost advantages</strong> let a company produce more cheaply than rivals, so it can undercut them on price or earn more at the same price. Sources include:</p><ul><li><strong>Scale:</strong> a giant retailer can negotiate lower prices from suppliers and spread costs over enormous sales.</li><li><strong>Location or resources:</strong> a quarry right next to a city saves on heavy transport costs that a distant rival must pay.</li><li><strong>Process:</strong> a manufacturing know-how that is hard to copy.</li></ul><p><span class="term" data-def="When a market is only big enough to support one or a few profitable companies, so new entrants would make returns unattractive for everyone.">Efficient scale</span> is subtler. Some markets are only big enough for one or two players. Think of a pipeline connecting two cities, or the only airport in a region. A second pipeline would split the traffic and leave both unprofitable, so nobody builds it. The incumbent keeps a steady business that no rational rival wants to attack.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Moat 5: intangible assets', html: `<p>Some of the strongest moats are things you cannot touch:</p><ul><li><strong>Brands</strong> that let a company charge more than an identical-looking product. People pay extra for the soda, sneakers or phone they trust. A brand only counts as a moat if it brings <em>pricing power</em> or loyalty, not just recognition.</li><li><strong>Patents</strong> give an inventor legal exclusivity, generally for 20 years from the filing date in the US. Drugmakers depend on them, and profits often drop sharply when generic copies arrive after a patent expires.</li><li><strong>Licenses and regulatory approvals</strong> that are hard to obtain, like permission to operate certain utilities, waste landfills or casinos.</li></ul><p>Remember from the balance sheet lesson: internally built brands usually do not even appear as assets. Some of the most valuable moats are invisible on paper.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "A famous logo is not automatically a moat. The test is whether customers pay more or stay loyal because of it." } },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each example to its main moat source.', pairs: [ { left: 'A drug protected from copies until 2033', right: 'Intangible asset (patent)' }, { left: 'A marketplace where all the buyers and sellers already are', right: 'Network effect' }, { left: 'The only gas pipeline serving a small region', right: 'Efficient scale' }, { left: 'A retailer whose huge size gets it the lowest supplier prices', right: 'Cost advantage' }, { left: 'Payroll software holding years of company data', right: 'Switching costs' } ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'How to spot a moat in the numbers', html: `<p>Every company claims to have a moat in its investor presentation. The numbers tell you whether it is real:</p><ul><li><strong>High returns on capital, sustained for many years.</strong> If ROIC stays well above peers through good times and bad, something is keeping competitors out.</li><li><strong>Stable or rising margins</strong> while rivals' margins shrink.</li><li><strong>Pricing power.</strong> Can the company raise prices without losing many customers? Buffett has said that pricing power is one of the most important things to judge when evaluating a business.</li><li><strong>Loyal customers,</strong> visible in metrics like retention rates or repeat purchases.</li></ul><p>A moat that does not show up in returns on capital is mostly a story. And stories are cheap.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Ortiz Bakeries raises wholesale bread prices by 5% and loses only 2% of its unit sales. By about what percent does wholesale revenue change?', answer: 2.9, tolerance: 0.1, unit: '%', explain: 'New revenue = 1.05 x 0.98 = 1.029 of the old revenue, about +2.9%. Losing few customers after a price rise is a sign of pricing power, at least for now.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Moats erode', html: `<p>No moat is permanent. Technology, regulation and customer habits change, and moats that looked unbeatable have dried up:</p><ul><li><strong>Kodak</strong> dominated photographic film for most of the 20th century. Digital photography erased that business, and Kodak filed for bankruptcy in 2012, despite an employee having built an early digital camera back in the 1970s.</li><li><strong>Newspapers</strong> once had local monopolies on classified ads. The internet moved those ads to free or cheaper websites.</li><li><strong>Video rental chains</strong> like Blockbuster lost out to mail-order DVDs and streaming. Blockbuster filed for bankruptcy in 2010.</li></ul><p>Patents expire. Network effects can reverse. Brands can lose their shine. A value investor's job is not just to find a moat, but to keep asking whether it is <em>widening or narrowing</em>.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Paying a premium price for a moat that is quietly drying up is one of the most expensive mistakes in investing. Re-check the moat every year." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A company with an excellent, popular product automatically has an economic moat.', answer: false, explain: 'Great products can be copied. A moat is something that stops rivals from copying or competing away the profits, like network effects, switching costs, cost advantages, intangibles or efficient scale.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Economic moat', back: 'A durable advantage that protects profits from competitors.' }, { front: 'Network effect', back: 'A product gets more valuable as more people use it.' }, { front: 'Switching costs', back: 'The pain of changing providers, which keeps customers loyal.' }, { front: 'Efficient scale', back: 'A market only big enough for one or a few profitable players.' }, { front: 'Pricing power', back: 'The ability to raise prices without losing many customers.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Why do most companies fail to keep very high returns on capital for long?', options: ['Taxes rise automatically', 'Competitors are attracted by high profits and compete them away', 'Stock prices fall each year', 'The SEC caps profits'], answer: 1, explain: 'High returns invite new rivals. Without a moat, competition pushes returns back toward average.' },
          { q: 'A payment card network becomes more useful as more stores accept it and more shoppers carry it. Which moat is this?', options: ['Network effect', 'Patent', 'Efficient scale', 'Switching costs'], answer: 0, explain: 'Each new user on either side makes the network more valuable to the other side, a classic network effect.' },
          { q: 'Which evidence best suggests a company really has a moat?', options: ['Its CEO says so on earnings calls', 'Its stock rose a lot last year', 'It has a famous logo', 'It has earned returns on capital well above peers for many years'], answer: 3, explain: 'Sustained high returns on capital, especially through tough times, are the clearest numeric sign that competitors are being kept out.' },
          { q: "What typically happens to a drugmaker's profits on a drug when its patent expires?", options: ['They usually fall as cheaper generic copies arrive', 'They double', 'They are unchanged forever', 'The government buys the drug at the old price'], answer: 0, explain: 'Patents are a powerful but temporary moat. When they expire, generics usually cut prices and profits sharply.' },
          { q: 'Kodak, newspapers and video rental chains show that...', options: ['Moats only matter for tech companies', 'Brands always protect a business', 'Even strong moats can erode when technology and habits change', 'Bankruptcy only hits small companies'], answer: 2, explain: 'Each had a strong position that technology shifts wore away. Moats need ongoing re-checking.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
    {
      id: 'stocks-3-intrinsic-value-dcf',
      title: 'Intrinsic Value: A DCF You Can Actually Do',
      summary: 'A business is worth the cash it will produce, discounted back to today. Build a simple discounted cash flow estimate and see how sensitive it is to your guesses.',
      minutes: 16,
      icon: 'calculator',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'What is a business worth?', html: `<p>Suppose Ms. Ortiz offered to sell you her entire bakery. How would you decide what to pay?</p><p>You would not care much about the share price of other bakeries, or what the ovens cost new. You would ask: <em>how much cash will this business put in my pocket over the years, and what is that stream of future cash worth to me today?</em></p><p>That is the definition of <span class="term" data-def="The value of a business based on the cash it will generate over its life, discounted back to today. An estimate, not a fact.">intrinsic value</span>. The standard tool for estimating it is a <span class="term" data-def="Discounted cash flow: a valuation method that projects future free cash flows and converts them into today's dollars using a discount rate.">discounted cash flow</span> (DCF) model.</p><p>Professional DCF models can have hundreds of rows. You are going to build a "DCF-lite" with five ingredients, and you will discover something important along the way: the answer depends enormously on your guesses.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Intrinsic value sounds fancy. It just means: how much is all the future cash worth in today's money?" } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why future dollars are worth less', html: `<p>Would you rather have $100 today or $100 a year from now? Today, obviously. You could invest today's $100 and have more in a year. There is also inflation, and the risk the promise is broken.</p><p>So to compare money at different times, you <span class="term" data-def="Converting a future amount of money into its equivalent value today, using a required rate of return.">discount</span> future cash back to today:</p><p><span class="hl">Present value = future cash / (1 + r)^n</span></p><p>Here r is the <span class="term" data-def="The yearly return you require to make an investment worthwhile, used to shrink future cash into today's value.">discount rate</span> (your required yearly return) and n is the number of years away.</p><p>At a 10% discount rate, $110 arriving in one year is worth $110 / 1.10 = <strong>$100</strong> today. $121 arriving in two years is worth $121 / 1.21 = also <strong>$100</strong>. This is just compound interest run backward.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Compounding asks what today's money becomes. Discounting asks what tomorrow's money is worth now. Same math, opposite direction." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'At a 10% discount rate, what is $220 received one year from now worth today, in dollars?', answer: 200, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: '$220 / 1.10 = $200.' },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Leo is promised $1,000 in 5 years. Using an 8% discount rate, what is it worth today, to the nearest dollar?', answer: 681, tolerance: 1, unit: '$', explain: '$1,000 / 1.08^5 = $1,000 / 1.4693, which is about $681. Money five years away is worth about two-thirds as much today at 8%.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Picking the discount rate', html: `<p>The discount rate is the return you demand for taking the risk. Common approaches:</p><ul><li>Start from what you could earn elsewhere. If safe government bonds pay 4%, a risky stock should promise meaningfully more.</li><li>Use a higher rate for riskier, less predictable businesses, and a lower one for steady, durable ones.</li><li>Many individual investors simply use a fixed hurdle, like 8% to 12%, and stay consistent.</li></ul><p>This connects to something you saw in chapter 1. When interest rates rise, investors demand higher returns, so discount rates go up. Cash flows far in the future shrink the most, because they are divided by (1 + r) many times. That is why fast-growing companies, whose profits are mostly far away, often fall hardest when rates jump.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Investors raise the discount rate they use from 8% to 11%. What happens to the present value of a company\'s future cash flows?', options: ['It rises', 'It falls, with distant cash flows falling the most', 'It stays the same', 'It falls only for cash flows in year 1'], answer: 1, explain: 'A higher discount rate shrinks every future dollar, and the effect compounds with time, so far-off cash flows lose the most value.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Building the DCF-lite', html: `<p>Lumen Bikes produces <strong>$4.00 of free cash flow per share</strong> this year. Our assumptions: FCF grows <strong>8% a year for 5 years</strong>, the discount rate is <strong>10%</strong>, and after year 5 growth slows to <strong>2.5% forever</strong>.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>FCF</th><th>Today's value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>$4.32</td><td>$3.93</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>$4.67</td><td>$3.86</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>$5.04</td><td>$3.79</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>$5.44</td><td>$3.72</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>$5.88</td><td>$3.65</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Those five years add up to about <strong>$18.94</strong> today. But the business does not vanish after year 5. The <span class="term" data-def="The value of all cash flows beyond the forecast period, usually estimated with a steady long-term growth rate.">terminal value</span> captures everything after: year 6 cash divided by (discount rate minus long-term growth), which is $6.02 / (0.10 - 0.025), about $80.32 at year 5. Discounted to today, that is about <strong>$49.87</strong>.</p><p>Estimated intrinsic value: $18.94 + $49.87 = <span class="hl">about $68.81 per share</span>.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The tail wags the dog', html: `<p>Look again at those numbers. Of the $68.81, about <strong>$49.87, or roughly 72%</strong>, comes from the terminal value: cash flows more than five years away, based on a growth rate you basically assumed.</p><p>That is normal for DCF models, and it is humbling. Most of the "value" depends on what happens in a future nobody can see clearly. It is why long-lasting competitive advantages, the moats from the last lesson, matter so much. A business with a wide moat is more likely to actually deliver those distant cash flows.</p><p>Also notice the formula's danger zone. The terminal value divides by (discount rate minus long-term growth). If you set those two close together, say 10% and 9%, you divide by 0.01 and get a gigantic number. Long-term growth forever should be modest, often around the long-run growth of the overall economy, and always well below the discount rate.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "If most of your valuation comes from year 6 to forever, you are mostly valuing your own assumptions. Stay humble." } },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'intrinsic-value', props: { fcf: 4, growth: 8, discount: 10, terminal: 2.5, years: 5 }, caption: 'Start with the Lumen example, then nudge growth, the discount rate and terminal growth by one or two points. Watch how much the value per share swings.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Garbage in, garbage out', html: `<p>Here is the same Lumen model with just two inputs changed:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Discount rate</th><th>4% growth</th><th>8% growth</th><th>12% growth</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>9%</td><td>$67</td><td>$80</td><td>$94</td></tr><tr><td>10%</td><td>$58</td><td>$69</td><td>$81</td></tr><tr><td>11%</td><td>$51</td><td>$61</td><td>$71</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Reasonable-sounding tweaks produce values from about <strong>$51 to $94</strong>. Nearly double! Anyone can make a DCF say almost anything by nudging the inputs. Analysts who love a stock have been known to do exactly that, often without realizing it.</p><p>So the lesson is not "DCFs are useless." It is: use a <strong>range</strong>, be honest about inputs, and combine the DCF with a margin of safety. If the stock is cheap even under your gloomy assumptions, that is far more convincing than a single precise number.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If your DCF says a stock is worth $68.81, the stock is worth exactly $68.81.', answer: false, explain: 'A DCF is only as good as its inputs. Small changes in growth or discount rate swing the value a lot. Treat the output as a rough range, not a precise fact.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Run it backward', html: `<p>A clever trick is the <span class="term" data-def="Starting from the current stock price and solving for the growth the market must be assuming.">reverse DCF</span>. Instead of guessing growth to get a value, start with today's <em>price</em> and ask: what growth would justify it?</p><p>Say Lumen trades at $94. Looking at the table, at a 9% discount rate that price already assumes something like 12% yearly growth for five years. Now the question becomes concrete: do you believe Lumen can grow free cash flow 12% a year? If its history is closer to 5%, the market is asking you to pay for a lot of optimism.</p><p>Reverse DCFs are useful because they turn a vague feeling ("seems expensive") into a specific claim you can check against the business's record, its industry and its moat.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'happy', say: "Rule of thumb: if the price only works with heroic assumptions, the assumptions are the risk. Check what is baked in." } },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Intrinsic value', back: 'The present value of all the cash a business will produce.' }, { front: 'Discount rate', back: 'Your required yearly return, used to shrink future cash to today.' }, { front: 'Present value', back: 'What a future amount is worth today.' }, { front: 'Terminal value', back: 'The value of all cash flows after the forecast years.' }, { front: 'Reverse DCF', back: "Solving for the growth that today's price implies." } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'At a 10% discount rate, what is $121 received in two years worth today?', options: ['$110', '$121', '$100', '$90'], answer: 2, explain: '$121 / 1.10^2 = $121 / 1.21 = $100.' },
          { q: 'Which change would INCREASE a DCF estimate of intrinsic value?', options: ['Raising the discount rate', 'Lowering the expected growth rate', 'Shortening the life of the business', 'Lowering the discount rate'], answer: 3, explain: 'A lower discount rate shrinks future cash flows less, so their present value, and the estimate, rises.' },
          { q: 'In a typical DCF of a healthy company, a large share of the value often comes from...', options: ['The terminal value, covering cash flows after the forecast period', 'Year 1 cash flow alone', 'The current share price', 'Cash on the balance sheet only'], answer: 0, explain: 'Distant cash flows, summed in the terminal value, often make up most of the estimate, which is why long-term assumptions matter so much.' },
          { q: 'Why is setting long-term growth close to the discount rate dangerous in the terminal value formula?', options: ['It makes the value negative', 'The denominator gets tiny, so the value explodes', 'It is illegal under GAAP', 'It removes the first five years'], answer: 1, explain: 'Terminal value divides by (discount rate - growth). As they approach each other, the result balloons to unrealistic levels.' },
          { q: 'What is the best way to use a DCF estimate?', options: ['As a precise target price', 'As proof a stock will rise', 'As a rough range, combined with a margin of safety', 'Only for companies with no cash flow'], answer: 2, explain: 'Because outputs are so sensitive to assumptions, treat DCF results as ranges and insist on a discount to them.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
    {
      id: 'stocks-3-value-traps-growth',
      title: 'Value Traps, Growth Traps and GARP',
      summary: 'Some cheap stocks are cheap for a reason. Learn to spot value traps and secular decline, how growth and value styles differ, and the middle path of growth at a reasonable price.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'warning',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The bargain that kept getting cheaper', html: `<p>Dev, newly converted to value investing, finds <strong>Grayline Printing</strong>, a hypothetical company that prints phone directories and paper catalogs. Its numbers look amazing:</p><ul><li>P/E of just <strong>5</strong></li><li>Dividend yield of <strong>9%</strong></li><li>Trading at half its book value</li></ul><p>"Graham would love this," Dev says, and buys. Over the next two years, the stock falls another 40% and the dividend is cut in half.</p><p>What went wrong? Grayline's revenue had been shrinking about 8% a year for a decade, because fewer people want printed directories every year. The low P/E was not Mr. Market being moody. It was Mr. Market being <em>right</em>. Dev had walked into a <span class="term" data-def="A stock that looks cheap on valuation ratios but keeps falling because the business is in lasting decline.">value trap</span>.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "A low P/E on shrinking earnings is not a discount. It is a melting ice cube on sale. Check which way the earnings are heading." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Cyclical dip or secular decline?', html: `<p>The key question with any cheap stock: is the problem <strong>temporary</strong> or <strong>permanent</strong>?</p><p>A <span class="term" data-def="A temporary downturn tied to the economic cycle or a passing problem, after which the business can recover.">cyclical dip</span> is a temporary slump. A homebuilder's profits fall in a housing slowdown, but people will need homes again. An airline suffers in a recession, then flies full again. These can be real opportunities if the company can survive the slump.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="A long-term, structural shrinking of demand for a product, usually from technology or habit changes. It does not reverse with the economy.">Secular decline</span> is a long, structural shrinking of demand. Printed directories, film cameras, DVD rentals, fax machines. No recovery is coming, because customers are not coming back. Even a well-run company in secular decline usually just shrinks more gracefully.</p><p>The danger is that both look identical on a stock screener: low P/E, high yield, falling price. You can only tell them apart by understanding the business.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Which company is most likely experiencing secular decline rather than a cyclical dip?', options: ['A truck maker whose sales fell 20% in a recession', 'A hotel chain hurt by a temporary drop in travel', 'A steel producer during a construction slowdown', 'A maker of DVD players whose sales fall every year as streaming grows'], answer: 3, explain: 'DVD player demand is shrinking because of a lasting technology shift. The others depend on the economic cycle and can recover when conditions improve.' },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Grayline trades at $20 with EPS of $4 (P/E of 5). EPS then falls 30% a year for two years while the price stays at $20. What is the P/E now? Round to one decimal.', answer: 10.2, tolerance: 0.1, unit: '', explain: 'EPS: $4 x 0.7 x 0.7 = $1.96. P/E = $20 / $1.96, about 10.2. The "cheap" stock doubled in P/E without the price moving, because earnings melted.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Value trap warning signs', html: `<p>No checklist is perfect, but these signs should make you slow down:</p><ul><li><strong>Revenue shrinking for years,</strong> not just one bad year.</li><li><strong>The whole industry is shrinking,</strong> not just this company.</li><li><strong>Dividends larger than free cash flow,</strong> paid with borrowed money or savings.</li><li><strong>Heavy debt</strong> coming due while profits fall.</li><li><strong>Management blaming "temporary headwinds"</strong> year after year.</li><li><strong>Buybacks or acquisitions</strong> used to hide shrinking core sales.</li><li><strong>Assets that may not be worth their book value,</strong> like old equipment or inventory nobody wants.</li></ul><p>Book value can mislead in a declining business. Printing presses for directories nobody reads are not worth what the balance sheet says. That is how a stock can trade at half of book and still be expensive.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'History: the department store giant', html: `<p>Sears was once the largest retailer in the United States, the place where generations of Americans bought everything from tools to clothes, even mail-order houses. As shopping moved to discount chains and later online, its sales shrank year after year. Along the way the stock often looked cheap against its huge real estate holdings and famous brands. Sears Holdings filed for bankruptcy in 2018. Cheap can stay cheap, then get cheaper.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'The ten-year question', html: `<p>Before buying any "cheap" stock, ask one simple question: <strong>ten years from now, will more people or fewer people need what this company sells?</strong> If the honest answer is "fewer," the low price may be a fair reflection of a shrinking future. If it is "more," and the company can survive today's trouble, the low price might be a real opportunity.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Growth vs value styles', html: `<p>Investors often sort stocks into two styles:</p><ul><li><strong>Value stocks</strong> trade at low multiples of earnings, book value or cash flow. Often mature, slower-growing, sometimes out of favor.</li><li><strong>Growth stocks</strong> have fast-rising revenue or earnings and usually trade at high multiples, because investors pay up for that future.</li></ul><p>Which style wins has rotated over time. Growth stocks beat value stocks by a wide margin through much of the 2010s, led by big tech companies. In 2022, when interest rates jumped, value stocks held up much better while many growth stocks fell sharply. Nobody has reliably predicted these rotations.</p><p>Here is the twist: Buffett has argued that the two are not really opposites. Growth is simply one input in calculating value. A fast-growing company can be a bargain, and a slow one can be overpriced. What matters is price compared with all the future cash.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Growth vs value labels come from ratios. Real value comes from cash flows. A growing company at a fair price is still value investing." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'In value investing, a company\'s growth rate is irrelevant; only low ratios matter.', answer: false, explain: 'Growth directly affects future cash flows, so it is a core part of intrinsic value. Low ratios on shrinking earnings can be a trap, while a growing business at a fair price can be a bargain.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'GARP: growth at a reasonable price', html: `<p>Between deep value and pure growth sits <span class="term" data-def="Growth at a reasonable price: seeking companies with solid earnings growth whose valuation is not excessive relative to that growth.">GARP</span>: growth at a reasonable price. The idea is to look for companies growing earnings steadily, with real competitive strengths, whose valuation has not run far ahead of that growth.</p><p>Peter Lynch, who ran Fidelity's Magellan fund from 1977 to 1990, is often linked with this style and with the PEG ratio you met in chapter 2. A GARP investor might prefer a company growing 15% a year at a P/E of 18 over one growing 30% at a P/E of 80, or one shrinking at a P/E of 6.</p><p>The appeal: you are not betting on a miracle, and you are not betting on a corpse. The risk: growth forecasts can still be wrong, and a "reasonable" price is still a judgment call.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each stock description to the best label.', pairs: [ { left: 'P/E of 4, revenue falling 10% a year for a decade', right: 'Possible value trap' }, { left: 'P/E of 9 during a recession, strong balance sheet, demand likely to return', right: 'Cyclical dip' }, { left: 'P/E of 90, revenue growing 40%, priced for perfection', right: 'Expensive growth' }, { left: 'P/E of 20, earnings growing 15% a year, durable moat', right: 'GARP candidate' } ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Growth traps exist too', html: `<p>Value traps get the headlines among value investors, but growth investors have their own version. A <strong>growth trap</strong> is a wonderful company bought at a price that already assumes years of perfection.</p><p>Remember the three engines from chapter 1: earnings growth, multiple change and cash returned. If you pay a P/E of 80 and the multiple later settles at 30, earnings must nearly triple just for you to break even. Even if the business does great, the stock can disappoint for years.</p><p>So both traps come from the same mistake: ignoring the relationship between price and future cash. The value trap ignores the future. The growth trap ignores the price.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: "A great company and a great investment are two different things. The price you pay decides which one you got." } },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Value trap', back: 'A stock that looks cheap but keeps falling because the business is declining.' }, { front: 'Secular decline', back: 'A permanent, structural drop in demand for a product.' }, { front: 'Cyclical dip', back: 'A temporary slump tied to the economy or a passing problem.' }, { front: 'GARP', back: 'Growth at a reasonable price: solid growth without an excessive valuation.' }, { front: 'Growth trap', back: 'A great business bought at a price that assumes perfection.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What best describes a value trap?', options: ['A stock that is cheap because the business is in lasting decline', 'Any stock with a P/E under 15', 'A stock that rises right after you sell it', 'A growth stock with no earnings'], answer: 0, explain: 'A value trap looks inexpensive on ratios, but its earnings and value keep shrinking, so it keeps getting cheaper for good reason.' },
          { q: 'A stock has a P/E of 6, and earnings fall 50% next year while the price is unchanged. What is its new P/E?', options: ['3', '6', '9', '12'], answer: 3, explain: 'Halving earnings doubles the P/E at the same price: 6 x 2 = 12. Low P/Es on falling earnings are illusions.' },
          { q: 'Which is the clearest sign of secular decline rather than a cyclical dip?', options: ['Profits fell during one recession year', 'Demand has shrunk for a decade due to a technology shift', 'The stock fell 15% in a month', 'Interest rates rose'], answer: 1, explain: 'Long-running, structural demand loss, often from new technology, is the mark of secular decline.' },
          { q: 'Which statement reflects the GARP approach?', options: ['Only buy stocks with P/E under 5', 'Buy the fastest growers at any price', 'Seek solid growth at a valuation that is not excessive relative to that growth', 'Avoid all companies that grow'], answer: 2, explain: 'GARP sits between deep value and pure growth, looking for durable growth without paying a heroic multiple.' },
          { q: 'Why can a wonderful, fast-growing company still be a poor investment?', options: ['Fast growth always ends in bankruptcy', 'Its share price may already assume more growth than it can deliver', 'Growth companies cannot be valued', 'Regulators cap their returns'], answer: 1, explain: 'If the price bakes in perfection, even strong growth can lead to weak returns when the multiple shrinks.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
    {
      id: 'stocks-3-value-checklist',
      title: 'Your Value Investing Checklist',
      summary: 'Pull it all together: circle of competence, management and capital allocation, insider ownership, smart buybacks, and the patience that makes value investing work.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'trophy',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why pilots use checklists', html: `<p>Airline pilots with thousands of hours of experience still run through a checklist before every takeoff. Not because they are bad pilots, but because even experts forget things when they are excited, rushed or overconfident.</p><p>Investors are the same. When you fall in love with a company's story, you skip steps. You ignore the debt. You forget to check the share count. Charlie Munger, Warren Buffett's longtime partner at Berkshire Hathaway, was a big believer in checklists for exactly this reason: they protect you from your own enthusiasm.</p><p>This lesson builds your value investing checklist, pulling together everything from this unit so far: what a share is, how to read the statements, valuation and quality ratios, moats, intrinsic value and margin of safety. Plus a few new ideas about the people running the business and the person doing the investing: you.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "A checklist is not boring. It is how you stop excitement from making your decisions for you." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Stay inside your circle of competence', html: `<p>Buffett and Munger talked constantly about the <span class="term" data-def="The set of businesses and industries you understand well enough to judge their future with reasonable confidence.">circle of competence</span>. Everyone understands some businesses well and others barely at all. Their point was that the size of your circle matters less than knowing where its edge is, and staying inside it.</p><p>Maya works in a café. She knows how coffee prices move, what customers complain about, how many pastries get thrown out each night, and why some cafés thrive while others close within a year. That is a real edge when she studies a coffee chain.</p><p>Leo works in logistics software. He understands why customers stick with a system once their warehouse runs on it.</p><p>Neither of them understands biotech drug trials or offshore oil drilling. That does not mean those are bad investments. It means they are someone else's game. A simple test: can you explain how the company makes money, and what could break it, in a few sentences?</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Leo is excited about a tiny biotech company with one experimental drug. He cannot explain how its trials work or what its odds are. What does circle-of-competence thinking suggest?', options: ['Buy a big position, since the upside is huge', 'Pass, or learn far more first, because it is outside what he understands', 'Buy because biotech always beats the market', 'Copy whatever an online forum recommends'], answer: 1, explain: 'If he cannot judge the business, he cannot estimate its value or a margin of safety. Staying inside your circle, or expanding it slowly, avoids blind bets.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Judge the capital allocators', html: `<p>Every year a profitable company has cash to deploy. Management's choices about it, called <span class="term" data-def="How management decides to use the company's cash: reinvesting, acquisitions, paying down debt, dividends or buybacks.">capital allocation</span>, can create or destroy enormous value. The options:</p><ol><li><strong>Reinvest</strong> in the business: new stores, products, equipment.</li><li><strong>Acquire</strong> other companies.</li><li><strong>Pay down debt.</strong></li><li><strong>Pay dividends.</strong></li><li><strong>Buy back shares.</strong></li></ol><p>None is always right. Reinvesting is great when returns on capital are high, like Ms. Ortiz opening a third shop that earns 20%. It is terrible when it means pouring money into a dying business. Acquisitions often disappoint, especially big ones paid for with lots of debt or overpriced stock.</p><p>Read several years of shareholder letters and see what management said it would do, then check what actually happened. Did past acquisitions work out, or show up later as goodwill write-downs?</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Skin in the game', html: `<p>You want the people running the company to think like owners, ideally because they <em>are</em> owners. Meaningful <span class="term" data-def="Shares of the company held by its executives, directors and founders.">insider ownership</span>, bought with their own money or built up over many years, aligns their interests with yours.</p><p>Things to check:</p><ul><li><strong>How much stock do executives own</strong> compared with their yearly pay? The proxy statement lists it.</li><li><strong>How is pay structured?</strong> Bonuses tied to EPS can encourage short-term buybacks. Pay tied to long-term returns on capital or per-share value is usually healthier.</li><li><strong>Insider trades.</strong> Executives must generally report their trades on a <strong>Form 4</strong> with the SEC within two business days, so anyone can see them on EDGAR.</li></ul><p>Peter Lynch made a famous observation along these lines: insiders sell for all sorts of reasons, like taxes, a house or diversification, but they generally buy for only one, because they think the stock will rise.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "One insider sale tells you little. Several executives buying with their own money at the same time is worth a closer look." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'Any time an executive sells shares, it means they expect the stock to fall.', answer: false, explain: 'Insiders sell for many reasons: taxes, diversification, buying a house, or pre-scheduled trading plans. Insider buying, especially by several people at once, tends to be a more meaningful signal.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Buybacks at the right price', html: `<p>In chapter 1 you learned buybacks can help or hurt. Now you have the tools to judge them: compare the buyback price with intrinsic value.</p><p>Buffett has said Berkshire Hathaway buys back its own shares only when the price is below a conservative estimate of their value and the company has plenty of cash left over. That is the right test. A buyback below value transfers wealth to the owners who stay. Above value, it transfers wealth to the ones who sell.</p><p>Picture a company worth <strong>$600 million</strong>, or $60 a share, with 10 million shares. If it spends $40 million buying 1 million shares at <strong>$40</strong>, the remaining 9 million shares share $560 million of value: about <strong>$62.22</strong> each. Owners who stayed got richer. If it instead paid <strong>$80</strong> a share, it would spend $80 million, leaving $520 million for 9 million shares: about <span class="down">$57.78</span> each. Same buyback, opposite result.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'A company worth $1 billion has 20 million shares ($50 each). It spends $60 million to buy back 2 million shares at $30. What is the intrinsic value per remaining share, in dollars? Round to two decimals.', answer: 52.22, tolerance: 0.02, unit: '$', explain: 'Remaining value: $1,000M - $60M = $940M. Remaining shares: 18M. $940M / 18M is about $52.22, up from $50, because the shares were bought below value.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Patience and temperament', html: `<p>The hardest part of value investing is not math. It is temperament.</p><p>Cheap stocks are usually cheap because they are unloved, and they can stay unloved for years. You may watch flashier stocks soar while yours sits still. Buffett has observed that the stock market tends to move money from impatient people to patient ones. Munger joked that a big part of investing success comes from sitting on your hands, waiting for a truly good opportunity instead of acting constantly.</p><p>Munger also loved a trick called <strong>inversion</strong>: instead of only asking "how could this investment succeed?", ask "how could it fail, badly?" Then check whether any of those failure paths look likely. Too much debt? A shrinking market? A moat being attacked?</p><p>Finally, admit mistakes quickly. If the facts behind your thesis change, change your mind, even if it means selling at a loss.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Value investing can lag the market for years at a stretch. If you need the money soon or cannot stand looking wrong, size accordingly." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The checklist', html: `<h4>The business</h4><ul><li>Can I explain how it makes money, in plain words?</li><li>Is it inside my circle of competence?</li><li>Does it have a moat, and is it widening or narrowing?</li><li>Will more or fewer people need its product in ten years?</li></ul><h4>The numbers</h4><ul><li>Are revenue and free cash flow growing, and do profits turn into cash?</li><li>Are margins and returns on capital strong and stable versus peers?</li><li>Can it survive a bad year: manageable debt, good interest coverage?</li><li>Is the share count shrinking, flat or climbing?</li></ul><h4>The people</h4><ul><li>Do managers own meaningful stock, and is their pay sensible?</li><li>Have past capital allocation decisions worked out?</li></ul><h4>The price</h4><ul><li>What is my intrinsic value range, under gloomy and hopeful assumptions?</li><li>Is there a real margin of safety below the gloomy case?</li><li>How could this go badly wrong?</li></ul>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each checklist question to the idea it comes from.', pairs: [ { left: 'Can I explain how it makes money?', right: 'Circle of competence' }, { left: 'Is the price well below my value estimate?', right: 'Margin of safety' }, { left: 'What stops rivals copying it?', right: 'Economic moat' }, { left: 'How could this fail badly?', right: 'Inversion' }, { left: 'Do managers own meaningful stock?', right: 'Skin in the game' } ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'A checklist is not a buy signal', html: `<p>Even a stock that passes every item can fall, sometimes a lot, and stay down. Businesses meet surprises nobody saw coming. That is why value investors diversify, size positions carefully and never invest money they will need soon. Many people use these ideas mainly to understand what they own inside broad index funds, and that is a perfectly smart way to apply them. This lesson is education, not a recommendation to buy any security.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Circle of competence', back: 'The businesses you understand well enough to judge.' }, { front: 'Capital allocation', back: 'How management uses cash: reinvest, acquire, repay debt, dividends, buybacks.' }, { front: 'Insider ownership', back: 'Stock held by executives and directors. Aligns their interests with owners.' }, { front: 'Form 4', back: 'The SEC filing that reports insider trades, generally within two business days.' }, { front: 'Inversion', back: 'Asking how an investment could fail, then checking for those risks.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What does staying inside your circle of competence mean?', options: ['Only buying companies in your home town', 'Investing only in businesses you understand well enough to judge', 'Owning exactly ten stocks', 'Avoiding all technology companies'], answer: 1, explain: 'The circle is about understanding, not geography or sector. Knowing where its edge is matters more than its size.' },
          { q: 'A company worth $50 a share buys back stock at $75. What happens to the value per share for owners who keep their shares?', options: ['It falls, because the company overpaid', 'It rises, because all buybacks help', 'It stays the same', 'It doubles'], answer: 0, explain: 'Paying more than intrinsic value transfers wealth from remaining owners to those who sold.' },
          { q: 'Which is generally considered the more meaningful insider signal?', options: ['One executive selling some shares', 'Executives receiving stock grants', 'Several insiders buying shares with their own money', 'The CEO giving an upbeat interview'], answer: 2, explain: 'Insiders sell for many personal reasons, but buying with their own money usually signals confidence in the business.' },
          { q: 'What is inversion, as Munger used it?', options: ['Buying stocks that have fallen the most', 'Reversing a stock split', 'Selling every losing position', 'Asking how an investment could fail badly, then checking for those risks'], answer: 3, explain: 'Inversion flips the question from how to win to how to avoid losing, which surfaces risks enthusiasm might hide.' },
          { q: 'Why is patience central to value investing?', options: ['Because cheap, unloved stocks can take years for price to reflect value', 'Because the SEC requires holding periods', 'Because buying and selling is illegal for value investors', 'Because value stocks always rise within a month'], answer: 0, explain: 'The gap between price and value can persist for a long time. Patience, and not being forced to sell, lets the thesis play out.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  bossQuestions: [
    { q: 'Your DCF for Lumen Bikes gives $60 a share under gloomy assumptions and $90 under hopeful ones. You insist on a 25% margin of safety below the gloomy case. What is the most you would pay?', options: ['$67.50', '$60.00', '$45.00', '$52.50'], answer: 2, explain: 'Anchor on the gloomy case for safety: $60 x (1 - 0.25) = $45. Using the hopeful case would build your margin on optimism.' },
    { q: 'A stock has a P/E of 6 and a 10% dividend yield. Revenue has fallen about 9% a year for eight years as its product goes obsolete, dividends exceed free cash flow, and a large loan comes due next year. What is the best description?', options: ['A classic margin-of-safety bargain', 'A likely value trap in secular decline', 'A cyclical dip that will recover with the economy', 'A GARP candidate'], answer: 1, explain: 'Long-running revenue decline from obsolescence, an unsustainable dividend and looming debt are value-trap signs. The low P/E reflects a shrinking future.' },
    { q: 'A company worth $2 billion ($40 a share, 50 million shares) spends $250 million buying back 5 million shares at $50. What is the intrinsic value per remaining share?', options: ['About $38.89', '$40.00', 'About $44.44', '$50.00'], answer: 0, explain: 'Remaining value: $2,000M - $250M = $1,750M. Remaining shares: 45M. $1,750M / 45M is about $38.89. Buying above value hurt the owners who stayed.' },
    { q: 'In a DCF, free cash flow in the first year after the forecast period is $5 per share. With a 9% discount rate and 3% long-term growth, what is the terminal value at the end of the forecast period?', options: ['$41.67', '$55.56', '$166.67', 'About $83.33'], answer: 3, explain: 'Terminal value = next-year FCF / (discount rate - growth) = $5 / (0.09 - 0.03) = $5 / 0.06, about $83.33. It must still be discounted back to today.' },
    { q: 'A company has earned ROIC around 25% for 15 years while competitors earn about 8%, and customers rarely leave after adopting its software. It trades at 60 times earnings. Which conclusion is most reasonable?', options: ['There is good evidence of a moat, but you still need a value estimate and margin of safety before calling it a bargain', 'A moat this strong means any price is fine', 'High ROIC proves the stock will beat the market', 'Switching costs never last, so the business has no value'], answer: 0, explain: 'Sustained high ROIC and sticky customers suggest a real moat from switching costs. But a great business at too high a price can still be a poor investment.' },
  ],
};
